WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.870
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:11.150
Today we are going to learn how to take any
shape in the real world, make a digital copy

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:15.490
of it, and place it into our virtual world.

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:20.050
These can be done through something that we
call differentiable rendering.

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:21.940
What is that?

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:28.640
Well, simple, we take a photograph, and find
a photorealistic material model that we can

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:32.310
put into our light simulation program that
matches it.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:39.790
What this means is that now, we can essentially
put this real material into a virtual world.

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:46.300
This work did very well with materials, but,
it did not capture the geometry.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:53.580
This other work is from Wenzel Jakob’s group
and it jointly found the geometry and material

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.150
properties.

00:00:55.150 --> 00:01:01.000
Seeing these images gradually morph into the
right solution is an absolutely beautiful

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:02.000
sight.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.420
But, as you see high-frequency details were
not as good.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:09.600
You see here - these details are gone.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.140
So, where does this put us?

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:19.250
If we need only the materials, we can do really
well, but then, no geometry.

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:27.150
If we wish to get the geometry and the materials,
we can use this, but we lose a lot of detail.

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:29.590
There seems to be no way out.

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:31.310
Is there a solution for this?

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:37.750
Well, Wenzel Jakob’s amazing light transport
simulation group is now back with guns blazing!

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:40.580
Let’s see what they were up to.

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:45.770
Let’s try to reproduce a 3D geometry from
a bunch of triangles.

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:47.860
Let’s see….and.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:48.900
Ouch.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:52.040
Not good, right?

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:54.230
So what is the problem here?

00:01:54.230 --> 00:02:01.970
The problem is that we have tons of self-intersections,
leading to a piece of tangled mesh geometry.

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:03.810
This is incorrect.

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:05.120
Not what we are looking for.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:12.090
Now, let’s try to improve this apply a step
that we call regularization, this guides the

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:15.800
potential solutions towards smoother results.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:19.110
Sounds good, hoping that this will do the
trick.

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:21.500
Let’s have a look together.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:24.630
So, what do you think?

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:30.450
Better, but there are some details that are
lost, and my main issue is that the whole

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:33.980
geometry is in fluctuation.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:35.330
Is that a problem?

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:36.830
Yes it is.

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:37.830
Why?

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:43.420
Because this jumpy behavior means that it
has many competing solutions that it can’t

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:44.460
choose from.

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:53.180
Essentially, the algorithm says, maybe this,
or not…perhaps this instead, no, not this.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:54.790
How about this?

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:56.819
And it just keeps going on forever.

00:02:56.819 --> 00:03:00.730
It doesn’t really know what makes a good
reconstruction.

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:06.610
Now, hold on to your papers, and let’s see
how the new method does with these examples.

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:08.550
Oh my!

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:16.170
This converges somewhere, which means that
at the end of the process, it settles on something.

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:19.300
Finally, this one knows what good is.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:20.300
Fantastic.

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:23.880
But now, these geometries were not that difficult.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:26.810
Let’s give it a real challenge!

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:28.680
Here comes the dragon.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:30.819
Can it deal with that?

00:03:30.819 --> 00:03:35.270
Just look at how beautifully it grows out
of this block.

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:39.910
Yes, but, look - the details are not quite
there.

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:41.890
So, are we done?

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:43.440
That’s it?

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:44.440
No!

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:45.440
Not even close!

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:47.220
But, do not despair for a second!

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:53.519
This new paper also proposes an isotropic
remeshing step, which does…this!

00:03:53.519 --> 00:03:57.100
On the count of one, two, three.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:03:59.620
Boom, we are done.

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:00.690
Whoa!

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:02.140
So good.

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:10.040
And, get this, the solution is not restricted
to only geometry, it also works on textures.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:17.059
Now, make no mistake, this is not super useful
in its current state, but it may pave a way

00:04:17.059 --> 00:04:21.109
to even more sophisticated methods two more
papers down the line.

00:04:21.109 --> 00:04:25.599
So, importing real geometry into our virtual
worlds.

00:04:25.599 --> 00:04:42.139
Yes please!

00:04:42.139 --> 00:05:24.680
Thanks for 

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:28.039
watching and for your generous support, and
I'll see you next time!

